{"id": "q0187", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "n_q_chunks": 63, "onset_chunk": 63, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is B, 6.2 8 x 10^-6 m s^-2. To solve this problem, you need to relate the change in gravity to the displacement of a mass on a spring. The system is an oscillating mass-s pring system with a natural period of two seconds. You can use the formula for simple harmonic motion and connect it to the variation in gravity. By calculating the relationship between the period, amplitude, and the change in gravitational acceleration, you find that the minimum detect able change in g is approximately 6. 2 8 x 10^-6 me ters per second squared.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 36, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "q0192", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "n_q_chunks": 54, "onset_chunk": 44, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's go through these options. First, there' s no layer of gas on Io to protect it from impacts. So B is not correct. C says nearby moons' gravity prevent objects hitting I o. That doesn't make sense because gravity doesn't work that way. D mentions Io's magnetic field deflecting asteroids. While Io does have a magnetic field, it' s not strong enough to stop all impact s. E says Io had craters but they were buried in lava. That's actually true. I o has constant volcanic activity and the surface is renewed so quickly that old craters get covered up. F suggests the material doesn't leave visible craters. But Io's surface materials do form craters when hit, just not many are left visible. G talks about Ju piter's gravity attracting planetesimals away from Io. That's not accurate either. H claims it was too small for late heavy bombardment hits. But Io did experience impacts then. So the best answer is E: Io did have impact craters, but they've been buried under lava flows.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 25, "carrier_ended": null}
